%erg 0.1
Title: bootstrap.js cannot be loaded twice into one scope
Created: 2026-09-07
Author: sim

--- log ---
2026-09-06T22:34Z sim created
2026-09-06T22:34Z claude note Found while probing why the shipped 0.3.0 still showed message ids in the author's window. It was NOT the cause -- that was a stale toolbar button in a Zotero process which had been up three and a half hours across six install/remove cycles, and a clean restart rendered English immediately. The probe is kept because it reddens on something real that nothing else here watches.

--- body ---
## Context

Loading `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` a second time into a scope that has
already evaluated it throws at parse time:

    SyntaxError: Identifier 'closeJournalled' has already been declared

Reproduced with the same `vm` loading `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` uses:

    const ctx = vm.createContext({ Zotero: {}, Services: {} });
    vm.runInContext(src, ctx);   // OK
    vm.runInContext(src, ctx);   // SyntaxError

`var` and `function` declarations tolerate redeclaration; `const`, `let` and
`class` do not. The file mixes all of them, so it is single-load-only by
accident rather than by decision. `closeJournalled` is simply the first such
binding the parser reaches — it is not special, and fixing that one name would
only move the error.

## What is NOT established, and it is the whole question

**Whether Zotero ever re-runs `bootstrap.js` into a scope it has already used.**
A bootstrapped add-on is normally given a fresh scope per install/enable, in
which case this is latent and harmless. Nothing measured tonight says
otherwise, and the symptom that prompted the probe turned out to have another
cause entirely.

It is filed anyway because the failure mode is silent and total: a parse error
takes the whole file, so `startup()` never runs, no journal record is written,
and the window keeps whatever the previous instance left on screen. That is
indistinguishable from the stale-button case that actually happened tonight,
which is precisely why it is worth being able to rule out rather than argue
about. Ticket 0688 spent weeks on a disappearance whose cause stayed open
because nobody could tell two silent failures apart.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — the mixed declarations
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` — already loads the file through `vm`, so
  the second load is one line away from being a driven arm

## Actions

1. Settle the open question first, because it decides whether this is a fix or
   a note: instrument a build to record its own load count in the journal
   (`emit('startup', …)` already fires once per `initialize()`), install it,
   and disable/re-enable a few times in one Zotero session. If the record
   count rises without a restart, Zotero reuses the scope and this is a real
   defect on the recovery path the plugin's own UI recommends
   ("turn the add-on off, then on again").
2. If it reuses the scope: make the top-level bindings `var`, the way
   `sitter`, `journal`, `generation`, `timer`, `pulse`, `heartbeat` and
   `timers` already are — ticket 0695 converted those for the sandbox-exposure
   reason, and this is the second, independent reason to prefer `var` in this
   one file.
3. If it does not: keep the arm from the Test section as a regression guard and
   close, recording the answer. A "cannot happen" that nothing checks is how it
   starts happening.

## Test

Add an arm to `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` that loads the source twice into
one context and asserts the second load succeeds. It reddens today, against the
file as it stands, which is what makes it worth adding before the fix rather
than after.

## Verification

- [ ] The double-load arm reddens against current `bootstrap.js`
- [ ] It greens once the declarations are converted
- [ ] The load-count question of action 1 is answered in this log, from a real
      Zotero session rather than from reading Gecko's source
- [ ] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- Ticket 0695's `var` bindings stay `var`: the sandbox-exposure reason for them
  is independent of this one, and a future reader tidying them back to `const`
  would break the driven suites.

## Exit criteria

Either the declarations are converted and the double load is guarded, or the
question is answered against a real Zotero and the answer is recorded here with
the guard kept.
